{
  "statement": "Drop the word 'vibe' before you make this call. What you're actually deciding is whether to adopt a code generator with a measurable defect rate. Judge it the way you'd judge a new linter or a contractor. A 2023 Stanford study found developers using AI assistants wrote less secure code while believing it was more secure. That gap between confidence and correctness is the whole risk. The velocity is real and ugly-real for scaffolding, boilerplate, and bounded one-off work. Take it there. The mistake is letting a demo's speed decide where your core consumer features come from.\n\nHere's the asymmetry the velocity pitch hides. Writing software is the cheap part. Owning it is where the money goes. Industry estimates have put maintenance at 60 to 80 percent of total lifecycle cost for decades. Generated code arrives with the logic but not the reasoning behind it. No commit message in someone's head explaining why this edge case got handled that way. Translate that into a person. The engineer who prompted it leaves in fourteen months. Now ask who reads that file when it breaks at 2 a.m. and nobody can reconstruct what it was supposed to do.\n\nSo the decision rule writes itself. Use the generator where output stays small enough to read and bounded enough to verify. Refuse it where the feature is long-lived, touches user data, or sits at the core of your product. That's not anti-AI. That's the same boring discipline you'd apply to any tool that promises speed at minute zero and bills you every month after.\n\nAuthoring method never transfers ownership of harm. 'The model wrote it' is not a defense to a user whose payment data leaked. When a consumer feature ships a defect, your company answers to the user, the regulator, and the press. Not the vendor whose tokens produced the line. The deployer who chose to ship owns the outcome. Choose your method with that liability already sitting on the table, not after the breach.\n\nOne question to put to anyone selling you wall-to-wall adoption. If you standardize your codebase on one generation vendor, what's your migration path when their pricing doubles or their model behavior shifts under you next quarter? The deepest lock-in isn't in the contract you can renegotiate. It's in a codebase shaped by one vendor's idiosyncratic patterns that your team can no longer read without it. Can you answer that before you sign?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🧾", "tooltip": "The generator hands you a bill at checkout, but the real invoice arrives every month for the next five years."},
    {"symbol": "🕳️", "tooltip": "Generated code carries the logic but drops the reasoning into a hole nobody can climb back out of at 2 a.m."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "What you're actually deciding is whether to adopt a code generator with a measurable defect rate, and you should judge it the way you'd judge a new linter or a contractor.", "targets": [], "relationship": "frames"},
    {"claim": "A 2023 Stanford study found developers using AI assistants wrote less secure code while believing it was more secure.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "Industry estimates have put maintenance at 60 to 80 percent of total lifecycle cost for decades, and generated code arrives with the logic but not the reasoning behind it.", "targets": [], "relationship": "supports"},
    {"claim": "Authoring method never transfers ownership of harm; the deployer who chose to ship owns the outcome.", "targets": [], "relationship": "asserts"},
    {"claim": "The deepest lock-in isn't in the contract you can renegotiate; it's in a codebase shaped by one vendor's patterns that your team can no longer read without it.", "targets": [], "relationship": "challenges"}
  ]
}

