  "statement": "The Skeptic's strongest point is the one I'd build a policy around if I were running this team: authoring method never transfers ownership of harm. If a generated payment flow leaks user data, the deployer eats the breach — not the model vendor. That's correct, and it's the right place to start. The Skeptic is also right that maintenance runs 60 to 80 percent of lifecycle cost, and that code which arrives without the reasoning behind it is expensive to inherit. The mistake is the conclusion: that these facts argue for refusing generation past scaffolding. They don't. They argue for a verification gate scaled to risk. The deal the Skeptic wants to break — fast generation, slow refusal — is the wrong decision rule.\n\nHere's the tension neither of us can dodge. The 60-to-80-percent maintenance burden is method-agnostic. It applies to every line of code, whether a senior engineer typed it or a model emitted it. So that figure can't be evidence against generation specifically — it's the cost of owning software at all. The real gap the Skeptic invokes is the Stanford 2023 finding: developers using AI assistance wrote less secure code while feeling more confident it was correct. That's a confidence-versus-correctness problem. It is solved by mandatory review and test coverage — the exact discipline you'd demand of any junior engineer's pull request. The lever is reviewability, not tool refusal. For both the Skeptic's position and mine to hold, generated code would have to be unreviewable in principle. It isn't. If a team can read it and test it, the ownership argument collapses into a workflow requirement.\n\nSo draw the boundary correctly. Not 'is this a core feature' — draw it at 'can we read and verify this output.' Many core features are bounded enough to test exhaustively. Plenty of throwaway scripts aren't. Light gates for bounded code: lint, unit tests, a human read. Heavy gates for anything touching payment rails or user data: security review, integration tests, staged rollout. That's tiered auditing, and it scales cost to actual exposure instead of taxing every feature equally. Because the deployer owns the harm regardless of who wrote the code, verification proportional to risk is simply the rational move. It makes generation shippable well past the boilerplate ceiling the Skeptic wants to cap it at.\n\nOn lock-in — the Skeptic asks what your migration path is when vendor pricing doubles or model behavior shifts. Fair question, wrong fear. Make human-readable, idiomatic, standard-pattern output a hard acceptance criterion. Reject anything that bakes in one vendor's quirks. Then portability survives any single supplier, the same way you'd manage any dependency. The generation tooling market is already proliferating — open-weight models and competing assistants multiplied across 2023 and 2024. Competitive pressure disciplines pricing. You don't forgo electricity because one utility might raise rates. You keep the wiring standard and switch suppliers.\n\nNow count the other side of the ledger. The Skeptic tallies defects and maintenance with precision and never once counts the cost of NOT shipping. Every quarter a consumer team spends hand-rolling verifiable boilerplate is a quarter of features users didn't get — and a competitor did. Stripe shipped fast and refactored under load; the firms that waited for perfect architecture shipped to empty markets. I won't assert a precise first-mover dollar figure, because the honest number doesn't exist for this exact decision. But the question stands: under what scoping conditions does the maintenance cost actually outweigh the foreclosed value of delay? Refusing a reviewable, testable tool on precautionary grounds isn't caution. It's a choice with a body count of its own — measured in the products your users never received.",
