. Just name which dependency you are actually managing: not the supplier, your own atrophied understanding.\n\nThe charge that I never count the cost of not shipping — half of that lands and it is uncomfortable. Delay is a real ledger entry. Products users never receive are a real loss. So is the answer to net the velocity gain against zero downstream cost, which is what the Accelerationist did. No dollar figure on the delay. No dollar figure on the rework, the security remediation, the 2 a.m. reconstruction labor. The Harvard Business Review \"workslop\" reporting names the failure directly: content that looks like finished work but lacks the substance, so the next person pays to rebuild it. Shipped-faster is only a gain after you subtract that. And the body-count-of-delay charge is aimed at a refusal I never made — take the speed for scaffolding, boilerplate, bounded one-offs. The boundary is about long-lived code that touches user data, not blanket abstention.\n\nSo here is the question no test suite answers. The team passes review, passes tests, ships, and the feature breaks in a way the suite never anticipated. Name the human who reconstructs the reasoning. Not the code — the code is right there, green and useless. The reasoning. Who on the team holds it. And if the honest answer is \"nobody, it was generated and nobody traced the why,\" then reviewability did not substitute for the core-feature boundary. It just hid that the boundary was missing until the night you needed it.",
