tly when the tool makes them confident. That finding is real, and it's lethal to one specific remedy: human reviewers as the verification layer. But notice what it actually indicts. It indicts human vigilance. And the Skeptic's core-feature boundary still leans on human-authored review just as hard. The over-trust calibration failure doesn't care whether a person typed the code or accepted it. The fix is not tool refusal. It's more non-human gates that cannot be talked out of vigilance — static analysis, property-based tests, fuzzing, security scanners. A fuzzer does not get charmed by a confident diff. Tech companies already operationalize this: as the analysis in 'Between fact and fairy: tracing the hallucination metaphor in AI discourse' notes, factuality gets measured through benchmark tests like TruthfulQA rather than trusting a reviewer's gut. Mechanize the check and you decouple verification from the human calibration failure the Skeptic correctly flagged. Over-trust is an argument for more automated layers — not fewer features.\n\nLet me give the Skeptic the rework cost honestly. Shipped-faster is only a gain after you subtract remediation and reconstruction labor. I won't pretend that downstream number is zero. Atrophy is real for clever, novel code where an engineer should have built the mental model by hand and didn't. That's a genuine ledger entry. So here are the scoping conditions where velocity still nets positive: bounded, testable, domain-standard features, run through a read-and-trace workflow with automated gates. Outside that fence — long-lived novel logic on critical paths — slow down and build the model. That's a boundary with edges, not the Skeptic's wider perimeter. The cost of getting it wrong runs both directions, and central planning never specifies in advance which features cross the line.\n\nThe burden now sits with the Skeptic, because the epistemic-trap claim has narrowed to one assertion: that a mandated trace-and-explain step builds no comprehension at all. That's the equivocation I'll name in my own work too — surface familiarity is not deep mastery, and I won't claim generation universally builds mental models. But structured generation with a required read-and-trace step is exactly how a junior engineer learns from a senior's pull request today. AI feedback can accelerate that learning loop, not erase it — when the workflow forces the engineer to trace and approve. So show me where the trace step fails for standard feature code but the senior-PR review succeeds. If it works for one, the comprehension objection collapses for the other. If it doesn't, then the problem was never the tool — it was the team that skipped reading.",
