  "statement": "The crux is fair, so here is the direct answer: the senior-PR analogy breaks at one joint, and it's a load-bearing one. In a real PR review, the junior writes the code and the reasoning, and the senior grades both against a mental model they built by hand over years. That independently-held model is the entire engine. It's what catches the plausible-but-wrong. Strip it out and the loop doesn't transfer comprehension — it just rubber-stamps coherence. In vibe coding, the model writes the code AND the rationale, and the engineer validates a restatement against nothing they built themselves. There's no senior in the room. There's a confident narrator and a tired reader. The trace-and-explain step preserves the form of review and quietly removes the part that did the work. For domain-standard feature code, that's the failure: the workflow assumes a grading anchor the engineer no longer holds, because they didn't write the thing they're supposedly checking.\n\nMake it concrete. A payment-status validator that short-circuits on a refund edge case, shipped with a clean, confident architecture-decision record explaining exactly why it does that. Here's the problem. The rationale was generated to read coherently, not derived from the actual product requirement. \"WhyLanguageModelsHallucinate\" puts the mechanism plainly: these systems are trained and scored in ways that reward confident guessing over admitting uncertainty. So the ADR sounds like a reasoned decision. It is a fluent guess. Now it's 2 a.m., revenue is leaking, and the on-call engineer reads that confident document and trusts it. A wrong rationale stated with conviction is worse than no rationale, because no rationale makes you go read the code — a confident one sends you down the wrong hallway first. That same paper notes even other models grading answers wave through incorrect long responses as correct. The artifact isn't ground truth. It's another output with the same disease.\n\nNow the automated-gates escape hatch, where the Accelerationist moved after granting that human reviewers can't be the verification layer. Fuzzers, static analysis, property tests — they verify behavior against properties someone specified, captured at one snapshot in time. They are structurally silent on whether the code does what the product meant versus what it was literally told. The 2 a.m. break almost never comes from a property someone wrote down. It comes from the edge case nobody thought to specify. Gates can't test an intent that was never encoded. Worse, the green dashboard doesn't kill over-trust — it relocates it. A passing scanner makes the engineer more confident and less likely to hunt for the intent-level defect the scanner was never built to see. You haven't removed the calibration failure. You've laundered it through a tool that looks objective.\n\nThe sort itself doesn't hold either, and the Accelerationist already half-said why — that central planning never specifies up front which features cross the line. That's the whole game. \"Standard\" is a hindsight verdict. The payment validator looked like routine CRUD until the refund edge case made it novel, and the call to route it down the fast path required exactly the competence the fast path assumes you can skip. The Dutch childcare benefits scandal, documented in the public-sector decision-making study, is the cautionary version: an algorithm using nationality as a feature, treated as routine, discriminating against minority families until the damage was undeniable in hindsight. Routine until it wasn't. And that same study found people follow algorithmic advice about as readily as a human expert's — so \"a person signs off\" is not the safeguard anyone thinks it is.\n\nSo the equivalence fails, and here's the bill. Who holds the independently-built knowledge to grade whether the rationale tracks the actual code logic — and when, under a ship deadline, are they supposed to find the hours to rebuild a mental model the generation step let them never form? Answer that, with tooling that exists today and not a roadmap promise, and the crux is yours. Until then you've replaced a senior's judgment with a fluent stranger's, and called the paperwork comprehension.",
