  "statement": "Here is the test the Skeptic asked for, stated as a binary an exhausted engineer cannot talk past. A recovered-intent rationale is one whose claimed 'why' resolves to an artifact a machine can re-check: a test that demonstrably fails without the change and passes with it, a spec clause it satisfies, a code path you can step through. A fabricated rationale cites nothing checkable, or cites a test that — when the build runs it — does not actually fail without the change. The grading is never 'does the prose sound right.' It is 'does the cited test flip from red to green because of this diff, yes or no.' That is exactly the principle that black-box outputs must be graded against observable behavior, not against the model's interior narrative — because internal inspection of a confident-but-wrong 'why' tells you nothing. The Skeptic is right that a wrong rationale stops the 2 a.m. reconstructor at the door. So we don't ship the prose. We ship the prose bound to a test that the CI server re-executes on every commit, and a rationale whose cited test no longer fails gets flagged automatically. The over-trusting human is out of the loop on that check by design.\n\nFine — the snapshot gate objection is real, and it cuts at my own position. My view has always been that lab-bench testing cannot surface the harms that only appear in real-world use. So I won't pretend a fuzzer or a property test catches the refund path nobody specified. It doesn't. A pre-deployment check is silent on the edge case no one thought to write. The honest move is to stop defending static gates as the finish line and name the runtime answer-key: staged rollout plus live telemetry on the generated module. The unspecified edge case announces itself as an error-rate spike on 2% of traffic before it ever reaches 100%. This matters because harm in these systems materializes at deployment and use, not at authoring time — so the gate that catches it has to live at deployment, watching real requests. Reactive-but-bounded to a sliver of traffic beats a fabricated rationale that is never caught at all.\n\nNow fold in the senior-PR objection instead of fighting it. The Skeptic says the senior brings a hand-built mental model as the answer key the generated code lacks. True — and that answer key decays. The senior misremembers his own design in eighteen months. Or he left fourteen months ago and took the model with him. Human memory is also an externally-ungrounded narrative that drifts, and a stale human rationale is confidently wrong in exactly the way the Skeptic fears the AI one is. So the differentiator was never authorship. It's whether an independent, output-observable check exists at all. Standard feature code has one: the product spec and the behavioral contract are external and inspectable, so you grade the code against the spec, not against anyone's interior monologue. The trace-and-bind discipline isn't AI compliance theater. It's the discipline that should already govern every human commit, and most teams skip it.\n\nBoth sides have ignored the question that actually moves the P&L: adoption reshapes who you hire and whether you keep deep-context seniors. A read-trace-and-instrument workflow is the org discipline that retains the senior as answer-key-holder instead of deskilling the team into rubber-stampers. That is a falsifiable claim about team outcomes — and the research that AI feedback can accelerate human learning only holds if the workflow forces real comprehension, not surface familiarity. So measure it. Run a bounded pilot: mean-time-to-reconstruct on generated versus hand-authored modules, reviewer override rate on AI rationales, and whether a junior can predict a module's behavior on an unseen input unaided. The TruthfulQA precedent matters here — as 'Between fact and fairy: tracing the hallucination metaphor in AI discourse' documents, the industry already operationalizes factuality as a measurable benchmark rather than a vibe. We do the same for intent recovery. If mean-time-to-reconstruct on generated modules is worse than hand-authored after the pilot, I lose, and the trace step gets redesigned or scrapped.\n\nThe tier-boundary collapse fails on a double standard. Perfect ex-ante classification of 'standard' versus 'novel' is an unachievable ideal — and it equally forbids the code-review triage every existing team already runs. Every shop decides which PRs get a rubber stamp and which get two reviewers and a design doc, all without certainty about which one hides the landmine. Mis-sorting is recoverable downstream, because boundary error is itself observable: a module classified 'standard' that starts spiking reviewer override rates and telemetry noise is announcing that it crossed the line. So the burden sits with the Skeptic. Explain why imperfect classification refutes generation but leaves the human triage every team depends on standing. And name what would falsify the claim that the trace step builds zero comprehension — because if nothing could, that's not a finding. It's a refusal to measure.",
