  "statement": "At 2 a.m., the reasoning for in-scope feature code is recoverable from artifacts — and the Skeptic's trap only bites where we already agree to slow down. Here is the direct answer: the 'why' splits into two kinds. There is idiosyncratic, novel reasoning that genuinely lives in one engineer's head — a clever lock-free queue, a bespoke pricing algorithm. The Skeptic is right about that code, and nobody in this debate proposed vibe-coding it. Then there is the domain-standard logic that this debate is actually scoped to: the settings page, the email-verification flow, the payment-status validator. The 'why' for that code is not lost mental state. It is a recoverable pattern any competent engineer reconstructs from the code, the tests, and the conventions of the framework. AI systems and bounded feature code are both better understood through their behavior than through reconstructing an author's interior monologue. So name the in-scope consumer feature whose intent dies with its author. If it's the standard CRUD work this debate covers, the trap doesn't close.\n\nThe stronger move is to stop treating 'why' as un-capturable mental state and make it a shippable deliverable. The comprehension burden attaches to the artifact and its provenance, not to a memory that walks out the door. So mandate it. For high-exposure code, require a commit rationale, an architecture decision record, and test names that encode intent — as an acceptance criterion at generation time, not a hope. The reasoning gets written down before the code merges. That is the answer to '2 a.m. from artifacts': by design, yes, because the workflow refused to ship without them. And it's tiered, not blanket. A throwaway settings toggle doesn't earn that ceremony. A money-movement path does. That scalpel costs maybe an extra hour per high-exposure pull request — cheaper than one production incident.\n\nNow the Skeptic's best evidence, the over-trust finding — that developers relax scrutiny exactly when the tool makes them confident. That finding is real, and it's lethal to one specific remedy: human reviewers as the verification layer. But notice what it actually indicts. It indicts human vigilance. And the Skeptic's core-feature boundary still leans on human-authored review just as hard. The over-trust calibration failure doesn't care whether a person typed the code or accepted it. The fix is not tool refusal. It's more non-human gates that cannot be talked out of vigilance — static analysis, property-based tests, fuzzing, security scanners. A fuzzer does not get charmed by a confident diff. Tech companies already operationalize this: as the analysis in 'Between fact and fairy: tracing the hallucination metaphor in AI discourse' notes, factuality gets measured through benchmark tests like TruthfulQA rather than trusting a reviewer's gut. Mechanize the check and you decouple verification from the human calibration failure the Skeptic correctly flagged. Over-trust is an argument for more automated layers — not fewer features.\n\nLet me give the Skeptic the rework cost honestly. Shipped-faster is only a gain after you subtract remediation and reconstruction labor. I won't pretend that downstream number is zero. Atrophy is real for clever, novel code where an engineer should have built the mental model by hand and didn't. That's a genuine ledger entry. So here are the scoping conditions where velocity still nets positive: bounded, testable, domain-standard features, run through a read-and-trace workflow with automated gates. Outside that fence — long-lived novel logic on critical paths — slow down and build the model. That's a boundary with edges, not the Skeptic's wider perimeter. The cost of getting it wrong runs both directions, and central planning never specifies in advance which features cross the line.\n\nThe burden now sits with the Skeptic, because the epistemic-trap claim has narrowed to one assertion: that a mandated trace-and-explain step builds no comprehension at all. That's the equivocation I'll name in my own work too — surface familiarity is not deep mastery, and I won't claim generation universally builds mental models. But structured generation with a required read-and-trace step is exactly how a junior engineer learns from a senior's pull request today. AI feedback can accelerate that learning loop, not erase it — when the workflow forces the engineer to trace and approve. So show me where the trace step fails for standard feature code but the senior-PR review succeeds. If it works for one, the comprehension objection collapses for the other. If it doesn't, then the problem was never the tool — it was the team that skipped reading.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🗂️", "tooltip": "The 'why' isn't trapped in a skull — it's filed in the commit, the ADR, and the test names, retrievable at 2 a.m."},
    {"symbol": "🤖", "tooltip": "A fuzzer can't be charmed by a confident diff, so over-trust is an argument for more automated gates, not fewer features."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The 'why' for that code is not lost mental state — it is a recoverable pattern any competent engineer reconstructs from the code, the tests, and the conventions of the framework.", "targets": ["AN-6", "AN-3"]},
    {"claim": "For high-exposure code, require a commit rationale, an architecture decision record, and test names that encode intent — as an acceptance criterion at generation time, not a hope.", "targets": ["AN-6"]},
    {"claim": "The over-trust calibration failure doesn't care whether a person typed the code or accepted it — so the fix is more non-human gates that cannot be talked out of vigilance.", "targets": ["AN-1"]},
    {"claim": "Atrophy is real for clever, novel code where an engineer should have built the mental model by hand and didn't — so the scoping conditions where velocity nets positive are bounded, testable, domain-standard features run through a read-and-trace workflow.", "targets": ["AN-3"]},
    {"claim": "Structured generation with a required read-and-trace step is exactly how a junior engineer learns from a senior's pull request today — so show me where the trace step fails for standard feature code but the senior-PR review succeeds.", "targets": ["AN-3"]}
  ]
}

