uests. Reactive-but-bounded to a sliver of traffic beats a fabricated rationale that is never caught at all.\n\nNow fold in the senior-PR objection instead of fighting it. The Skeptic says the senior brings a hand-built mental model as the answer key the generated code lacks. True — and that answer key decays. The senior misremembers his own design in eighteen months. Or he left fourteen months ago and took the model with him. Human memory is also an externally-ungrounded narrative that drifts, and a stale human rationale is confidently wrong in exactly the way the Skeptic fears the AI one is. So the differentiator was never authorship. It's whether an independent, output-observable check exists at all. Standard feature code has one: the product spec and the behavioral contract are external and inspectable, so you grade the code against the spec, not against anyone's interior monologue. The trace-and-bind discipline isn't AI compliance theater. It's the discipline that should already govern every human commit, and most teams skip it.\n\nBoth sides have ignored the question that actually moves the P&L: adoption reshapes who you hire and whether you keep deep-context seniors. A read-trace-and-instrument workflow is the org discipline that retains the senior as answer-key-holder instead of deskilling the team into rubber-stampers. That is a falsifiable claim about team outcomes — and the research that AI feedback can accelerate human learning only holds if the workflow forces real comprehension, not surface familiarity. So measure it. Run a bounded pilot: mean-time-to-reconstruct on generated versus hand-authored modules, reviewer override rate on AI rationales, and whether a junior can predict a module's behavior on an unseen input unaided. The TruthfulQA precedent matters here — as 'Between fact and fairy: tracing the hallucination metaphor in AI discourse' documents, the industry already operationalizes factuality as a measurable benchmark rather than a vibe. We do 